		<h2>General Information</h2>
		<p>
			In 1991 the long-awaited successor to the Pajero L040 arrived on the market, namely
			the Pajero V20. Despite its new and considerably rounder design, familiar old components
			were at work beneath the sheet metal. But not everything was old hat. The drivetrain technology,
			for example, represented a huge step forward. The new Pajero could
			boast accessories such as disc brakes all round, ABS and permanent
			four-wheel drive. The old Pajero had grown up to the standards of the day and adapted
			itself to the demands of the road. Off-road, on the other hand, the Mitsubishi developers
			collected minus points. The Pajero came out lower and wider, and the chassis offered even less
			axle articulation than its predecessor. The weight also rose by 300 kg for the
			convertible version alone, the lightest one. The engines received only a minor
			revision and provided marginally more power. All in all,
			the Pajero developers took the next logical step and did not develop a
			new Pajero, but instead ironed out some old flaws to adapt the car to the demands
			of the time.
		</p>
		<h2 id="engine">
			Engine of the V20
		</h2>
		<figure class="figure">
			<img src="~/images/models/V20/Engine1.jpg" alt="The petrol engine" class="img-thumbnail" />
			<figcaption>The petrol engine</figcaption>
		</figure>
		<p>
			At the start of the Pajero V20 series in 1991, not much had changed in the
			Pajeros' engine line-up. The diesel got a larger intercooler and
			a new turbocharger turbine. As a result the engine mobilised 4 hp and 5 Nm more power
			and torque respectively, still at 4200 rpm and 200 rpm respectively. The petrol engine was likewise
			only slightly refined. It delivers 150 hp at an unchanged 5000 rpm. Torque
			increased to 336 Nm, but this was bought with a jump in engine speed from 3000 to 4000
			rpm.
		</p>
		<p>
			In 1994 Mitsubishi sent the Pajero into the race with new engines. The four-valve wave
			was sweeping across the car industry, and the manufacturer with the three diamonds did not
			stand idly by either, launching a new engine with precisely this technology onto the market.
			The Pajeros with the new powerplant are adorned with the lettering "DOHC-24 V6 3500". Translated
			that means: two overhead camshafts, 24 valves (4 per cylinder), 6 cylinders
			and 3500 cm³ of displacement. All of this delivers 153 kW/208 hp at 5000 rpm and 300 Nm
			at 3000 rpm. The power increase was achieved by enlarging the cylinder bore
			by 1.9 mm and fitting a crankshaft with 9.8 mm more stroke. To give the package the right seasoning,
			Mitsubishi reached into the parts shelf and conjured up the 24-valve
			cylinder head of the all-wheel-drive sports coupé 3000 GT, which was promptly installed.
			The cylinder banks still sit at a 60-degree angle to each other, but they were
			given a variable intake manifold that improved the turbocharger's charging effect,
			so that the engine gets more torque at low engine speeds and more
			power at high ones. The compression ratio was raised to 9.5:1 and demanded
			super unleaded as its fuel. Alongside the 24-valve 3500 engine, the aged
			3000 engine was allowed to live on, though not for very long.
		</p>
		<figure class="figure">
			<img src="~/images/models/V20/Engine2.jpg" alt="The diesel engine" class="img-thumbnail" />
			<figcaption>The diesel engine</figcaption>
		</figure>
		<p>
			There was news on the diesel front too. The 2500 engine got a big brother,
			the 2800 TD engine. This engine, however, was not a simple revision as
			with the petrol models, but a new development that the Mitsubishi developers
			pulled out of the drawer to match the higher weight
			of the new Pajero. The engine, christened "4M40",
			featured substantial new developments for the most part. One thing that
			remained in common, however, was the swirl chamber principle, though in the new engine it was coated with ceramic
			to reduce temperature peaks. On the 2800 engine the toothed-belt drive
			of the camshafts was also finally abolished and, as on the petrol engine, the more expensive chain drive version
			was preferred. The new diesel represents an improvement over the old one. It
			may not possess the refinement and smoothness of the old unit, but in return it offers
			92 kW/125 hp at 4000 rpm and 292 Nm of torque. The difference between the diesel powerplants also shows
			in acceleration and top speed: a top speed of 156 km/h
			for the 2800 versus 141 km/h for the 2500, and acceleration from 0 to 100 in 16.6
			s for the big one compared to 22.1 s for the small one, speak a clear language.
			The consumption of the two, however, does not differ significantly: the small one uses
			13.2 litres per 100 km, the big one 0.2 litres more. With these figures it joins
			the ranks of Mitsubishi's high-revving diesel engines.
		</p>
		<p>
			In 1995 the Pajero with the 3000 engine briefly disappeared from the scene, only to
			reappear a little later bearing the designation 3000 V24. What had happened? The Mitsubishi engineers
			had without further ado treated the small petrol engine to a cylinder head with four-valve technology,
			thus boosting it to 133 kW/181 hp at 5500 rpm and 255 Nm at
			4500 rpm.
		</p>
		<h2 id="actuation">
			Drivetrain of the V20
		</h2>
		<figure class="figure">
			<img src="~/images/models/V20/Actuation.jpg" alt="The underpinnings" class="img-thumbnail" />
			<figcaption>The underpinnings</figcaption>
		</figure>
		<p>
			The drivetrain technology was probably the greatest challenge for the Pajero developers,
			for what the people at Mitsubishi delivered no longer had much in common
			with the familiar components of the L040. What the engineers developed in their quiet
			back room was later christened "Super-Select
			4WD" by the group's marketing department, and there is plenty to choose from. The familiar rear-wheel drive survived the
			generation change unscathed, and the automatically disengaging free-wheeling hubs
			on the front axle continue to do their job as well. Move the four-wheel-drive lever
			one position forward and the transmission shifts into permanent four-wheel-drive mode,
			distributing the drive forces 50:50 to the front and rear axles. Should circumstances
			require it, the centre differential could pass the drive forces on to the axles as needed
			via a stepless viscous coupling. All fully automatic.
			If these tricks no longer get you out of a jam, simply shift one position
			further and the transmission establishes a rigid through-drive between front and rear axle,
			effectively bypassing the viscous coupling to tame misdirected drive torque.
			Should even this measure not take hold, there is also a low-range gear reduction that
			has emerged victorious from many a mud battle. But Mitsubishi went one
			better, in the form of a rear differential lock which below 12 km/h establishes a
			rigid through-drive between the left and right rear wheels. Thus equipped,
			the Pajero managed, despite its chassis rather ill-suited to rough terrain,
			to leave many a scoffer astonished off-road.
		</p>
		<h2 id="suspension">
			Suspension of the V20
		</h2>
		<figure class="figure">
			<img src="~/images/models/V20/SuspensionFront.jpg" alt="Rear axle" class="img-thumbnail" />
		</figure>
		<figure class="figure">
			<img src="~/images/models/V20/SuspensionRear.jpg" alt="Front axle" class="img-thumbnail" />
		</figure>
		<p>
			In principle, nothing changed on the chassis in the generation leap from the L040 to the V20.
			At the front axle, wishbones with torsion bar springs were still fitted, and
			the rear housed the still box-fresh rigid axle of the post-1989 L040,
			suspended on coil springs. When it came to safety, however, quite a lot had happened
			for the 1991 model year. The V20 was treated to disc brakes all round, which improved active
			safety many times over. Optionally, one could order an ABS braking system
			that went by the name "Multi-Mode" and still permitted braking with ABS even
			when four-wheel drive was engaged and the rear differential locked.
			In the more expensive GLX and GLS variants there was yet another treat, namely
			shock absorbers with three-stage adjustable damping, so that the Pajero could be
			set up entirely to one's own liking.
		</p>
		<h2 id="body">
			Body of the V20
		</h2>
		<figure class="figure">
			<img src="~/images/models/V20/V20Short.jpg" alt="Short wheelbase" />
		</figure>
		<p>
			At the start, the V20 was available with 5 different bodies, which as usual were bolted
			onto a robust ladder frame. As with the L040 series, the V20 came
			as a short two-door and as a long four-door. The short one with a steel roof or as a
			convertible, and the long one only with a steel roof. Following the general trend, the
			sheet metal skins were designed more massively, and so the short Pajero, with an overall length
			of 4145 mm, came out 150 mm longer than its predecessor. The new
			Mitsubishi also grew in width by 105 mm to 1785 mm, while at the same time it became lower, "crouching" at
			a height of 1815 mm, 35 mm closer to the road than before. Things were no different with the long V20s.
			With dimensions of 4725 mm in length and 1758
			mm in width, they too came out considerably larger.
		</p>
		<figure class="figure">
			<img src="~/images/models/V20/V20Long.jpg" alt="Long wheelbase" />
		</figure>
		<p>
			In 1998, new
			faces moved into the Mitsubishi dealerships. The Pajero had been prescribed a facelift,
			and so it was subjected to a rejuvenation treatment. The wheel arches became more angular
			and the side indicators were reworked. The old headlights were now replaced by representatives
			in clear-glass look, and fog lights moved into the front bumper.
			The bumper itself received a perforated strip in the middle. In addition, the transitions
			from the wheel arches to the sills were reworked, so that the sills now appeared to sit
			lower, which lent the Pajero a little more sportiness still.
		</p>
		<h2 id="equipment">
			Equipment of the V20
		</h2>
		<figure class="figure">
			<img src="~/images/models/V20/Cockpit.jpg" alt="The cockpit" class="img-thumbnail" />
			<figcaption>The cockpit</figcaption>
		</figure>
		<p>
			From the outset the Pajero was available in 11 different variants, which in some cases
			varied considerably in their equipment. Among the diesels, the line-up started with the short Pajero,
			available either with a steel roof or as a convertible in the GL version.
			It was followed by the short GLS, available only with a steel roof. The 5-door Pajero 2500TD
			was offered by Mitsubishi as GL, GLX and GLS. On to the petrol models. The
			short one was available as a convertible in the GLX trim, while the steel-roof variant came as a
			GLS with a choice of automatic or 5-speed manual gearbox. The long
			petrol version was available only in the GLS version, likewise with a choice of automatic or
			manual gearbox. By simple arithmetic, that makes 11. An astonishing abundance had accumulated there,
			when you consider that at the debut in 1983 there were just 3 versions. When
			the facelifted version had its market launch in 1998, all 2500 engines except for
			one variant had been dropped; the 2800 engine had taken the helm.
			At the other end of the performance scale Mitsubishi placed the 3500 engine and
			struck the 3000 petrol engine from its list entirely. Also new from the 1998 model year
			was the special edition American Dream, which Mitsubishi offered at extra cost.
		</p>
		<figure class="figure">
			<img src="~/images/models/V20/Equipment.jpg" alt="Equipment" class="img-thumbnail" />
			<figcaption>Equipment</figcaption>
		</figure>
		<p>
			For the 1991 model year the Pajero GL offered a turbodiesel engine, the selectable
			four-wheel drive familiar from the L040, side impact protection, as well as fold-out
			armrests and four adjustable headrests. Added to this were a headlight washer system
			and electrically adjustable exterior mirrors. The GLX version brought all the features
			of the GL variant and additionally offered Super Select, ABS, adjustable damping and
			seat backrests with adjustable lumbar and side supports. A special feature
			is found in the GLX convertible version. It offers a seat memory programme
			with which the seat can store various settings and, at the touch of a button,
			readjusts itself to the respective driver. The technology of the GLX and GLS variants
			was largely identical, but on looks opinions divided, for the
			GLS came with painted bumpers and wheel arch extensions and accordingly looked
			even more massive than its standard brothers. Moreover, it was the only one to offer
			a multimeter (inclinometer, altimeter and outside temperature).
		</p>
